@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@token = PASTE_ACCESS_TOKEN_HERE

# The product id used for the normal scenarios. Set it to one your catalogue
# actually has (GET /products shows them), then give it stock in the warehouse:
#
#   cd inventory-service
#   docker compose exec inventory-postgres psql -U postgres -d inventory \
#     -c "INSERT INTO stock (product_id, available, reserved) VALUES ('2', 10, 0) \
#         ON CONFLICT (product_id) DO UPDATE SET available=10, reserved=0"
@productId = 2

# A product the WAREHOUSE knows but the CATALOGUE does not. Used to force the
# "reserved, then the order failed to save" case. Create it with:
#
#   docker compose exec inventory-postgres psql -U postgres -d inventory \
#     -c "INSERT INTO stock (product_id, available, reserved) VALUES ('9999', 10, 0) \
#         ON CONFLICT (product_id) DO UPDATE SET available=10, reserved=0"
@orphanProductId = 9999

# =============================================================================
# Requirement 2 - the main application talking to the inventory service
#
# Paste an access token into @token above (get one from auth.http).
# Full walkthrough: docs/inventory-service/manual-testing-ar.md
# =============================================================================

### Product details carry the warehouse quantity
# The `stock` block comes from the inventory service over gRPC.
# It is null when the warehouse cannot be reached - the page still renders.
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### The product list carries stock too
# One batch GetStock call for the whole page, not one call per product.
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?page=1&limit=10
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### A normal order - reserves stock, then writes the order
# Expect 201. Re-read the product afterwards: available drops, reserved rises.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": {{productId}},
      "quantity": 2
    }
  ]
}


### CASE 1 - not enough stock
# Ask for more than the warehouse holds, but stay within the per-line limit of
# 1000 that requirement 1 enforces: a larger number is rejected with 400 by
# local validation before any gRPC call is made.
#
# Expect 409 with a "shortages" array naming the product, what was requested
# and what is actually left. Nothing is written, nothing is reserved.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": {{productId}},
      "quantity": 1000
    }
  ]
}


### CASE 2 - the inventory service is stopped or too slow
# Stop it first:
#   cd inventory-service && docker compose stop inventory
# Or make it slow (6s delay against a 3s client budget):
#   INVENTORY_ARTIFICIAL_DELAY_MS=6000 docker compose up -d inventory
#
# Expect 503 within about 3 seconds, never a hang:
#   stopped -> "The inventory service is unavailable. Your order was not placed..."
#   slow    -> "The inventory service did not respond in time. Your order was not placed..."
#
# Afterwards check the warehouse: the quantity must be unchanged. A reserve that
# timed out may still have succeeded on the server, so the application releases
# it; the tombstone then refuses the late reserve.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": {{productId}},
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ]
}


### CASE 2b - the catalogue still works while the warehouse is down
# Expect 200 with "stock": null. A product page without a quantity is far
# better than an error page.
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### CASE 3 - the reservation succeeded but saving the order failed
# @orphanProductId exists in the warehouse but not in the catalogue, so the
# reserve succeeds and the order write then fails.
#
# Expect 404 "Product with id 9999 not found", and - the point of the test -
# the warehouse quantity back exactly where it started, because the application
# released the reservation. The log line reads:
#   Released reservation <uuid> after a failure (released=true)
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": {{orphanProductId}},
      "quantity": 3
    }
  ]
}


### PROOF - the race for the last unit
# Set the product to a single unit first:
#   docker compose exec inventory-postgres psql -U postgres -d inventory \
#     -c "UPDATE stock SET available=1, reserved=0 WHERE product_id='2'"
#
# Then send this request many times AT ONCE. REST Client fires one at a time,
# so for a real race use the script instead:
#   npm run demo:req2 -- 30
#
# Exactly one gets 201; every other gets 409. Final state: available 0, reserved 1.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": {{productId}},
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ]
}


### Read the order that was created
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders?page=1&limit=20
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
